ions.\n\n---\n\n**3. Are birds present and interacting aggressively with the snowmobile?**\n\n→ **No.** A white bird (likely a ptarmigan) is shown being held by the vlogger after being hunted (frames 19–22, 26–28), but there is no visual evidence of birds interacting with or attacking the snowmobile.\n\n---\n\n**4. Does the snowmobile appear stationary or non-functional?**\n\n→ **Partially.** The snowmobile is often stationary in the frames (e.g., frames 11–12, 23–25, 29–30), but there is no visual indication that it is *non-functional*. The vlogger is seen handling equipment, packing the bird, and standing beside the machine — suggesting it is operational and simply parked.\n\n---\n\n**5. Is the snowmobile positioned in a way that suggests it cannot move backwards (e.g., stuck, blocked, or mechanical issue)?**\n\n→ **No.** The snowmobile is parked on flat or gently sloped snow-covered terrain. There is no visual evidence of it being stuck in snow, blocked by obstacles, or showing mechanical failure. The surrounding snow appears undisturbed around the tracks, suggesting no struggle to move.\n\n---\n\n**6. Do people show signs of distress or problem-solving related to the snowmobile?**\n\n→ **No.** The vlogger and child appear calm, focused on the hunt and packing the bird. The vlogger is smiling in several frames (e.g., 1, 2, 30) and shows no signs of frustration, confusion, or attempting to fix or move the snowmobile manually.\n\n---\n\n**Temporal Sequence Analysis (from hunting aftermath to snowmobile situation):**\n\n- The hunting sequence concludes around frame 22, where the bird is shown being held.\n- From frame 23 onward, the vlogger is seen returning to the snowmobile, packing the bird into a bag (frames 26–28), and then standing beside the snowmobile (frames 29–30).\n- There is no transition or sequence that implies a problem with the snowmobile’s reverse gear or mobility. The actions are routine: hunting → retrieving → packing → preparing to leave.\n\n---\